F('ex-temperley-lieb-order-three-root-boundary','At ell3 the nonzero projector p2=1-U1 has trace0, while TL2=C+C and the trace radical is its one-dimensional block.',C('The loop is1, but the projector trace[3] is0 and the explicit all-ones trace pairing has kernel C(1-U1).'),C('U1 is the lone rank-two generator, with U1 squared=U1 and complementary nonzero idempotent1-U1.'),C('The trace form is degenerate although the algebra is semisimple, separating trace radical from Jacobson radical.'),C('The rank n2 is exactly the first boundary ell-1; the nonzero boundary projector is removed only by the negligible quotient.'),N('Both basis diagrams, complementary idempotents and the kernel vector are displayed explicitly.'),'The witness is the positive two-strand first boundary at ell3, not the empty path algebra.')
F('ex-temperley-lieb-ell-four-and-five-fusion','The finite retained-label fusion tables are obtained by substituting ell4 and ell5 into the complete truncated rule.',N('The loop values at ell4 and ell5 are nonzero; neither a loop0 theory nor a zero-order root is part of these two examples.'),C('Label0 is the unit in both tables, and the fundamental label1 follows the truncated nearest-neighbor products.'),C('Terms above the allowed level are absent by truncation; no undefined boundary projector or vanishing quantum denominator is divided.'),C('The tables include the top retained labels2 for ell4 and3 for ell5 and enforce the upper truncated fusion bound.'),N('There are finitely many specified label pairs, and the table entries follow the explicit inequalities without choosing fusion intertwiners.'),'Both finite categories have their tensor-unit label0; this is a unit object, not an empty category.')
A('def-temperley-lieb-type-a-path-spaces-and-matrix-unit-tower','The graph, finite paths, endpoint blocks and prefix-extension map are defined; adjacency is stipulated by absolute difference1, not proved as a theorem characterizing path towers.',empty=C('The Definition explicitly permits empty endpoint path sets and omits their zero matrix summands. At n0 the sole path(0) instead gives K0=C.'),zero=C('An unreachable endpoint gives V_(n,a)=0 and no algebra summand; label0 itself is the starting vertex, not a discarded zero object.'),one=C('At n1 there is the single path(0,1), since ell>=3; its endpoint block is C.'),degenerate=C('At ell3 the graph has only vertices0,1, so each path alternates and every level has one scalar block.'),endpoints=C('Steps below0 or above ell-2 are excluded from the path set, so prefix extension sums only over actual neighbors.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The path basis and all matrix units are canonically indexed by finite vertex sequences; no basis for an abstract vector space is selected.')})
A('thm-temperley-lieb-type-a-path-space-tower-branching','Deleting the last vertex partitions a path basis, and disjoint image coordinates prove injective unital prefix-extension maps.',empty=C('Empty endpoint path spaces contribute zero summands; the source level0 still has its one empty-length path and scalar identity.'),zero=C('Products of matrix units from different endpoint blocks vanish and the proof explicitly preserves those zero products.'),one=C('The unique n0 path extends to(0,1); the scalar inclusion K0 to K1 is unital and injective.'),degenerate=C('Even for ell3 each vertex has its one neighbor, so the proof of an injective map and multiplicity-one branching still applies.'),endpoints=C('At vertex0 and vertex ell-2 there is only one neighbor; outside vertices contribute no summand, as in the stated direct sum.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The injectivity proof selects a neighbor for each member of one finite path basis; such finite selections exist since every graph vertex has a neighbor, with no infinite-family choice.')})
F('def-xxz-open-chain-temperley-lieb-representation','The displayed local4-by-4 U(q), its ordered spin basis and H=-sum of the L-1 adjacent embeddings fix the open XXZ convention.',C('Loop delta0 occurs at q=±i, and the displayed U(q) still exists since q itself is nonzero.'),C('The tensor formula allows L2 with a single bond e1 and zero tensor powers of the identity at either end.'),C('The Definition does not assume self-adjointness for arbitrary unit-circle q; its generally complex diagonal entries remain as displayed.'),C('The generator range1<=i<L excludes a last-to-first bond and both exterior tensor exponents are nonnegative.'),N('The standard spin basis, Pauli matrices and finite tensor embeddings are specified data, so no arbitrary-basis choice is made.'),'The Definition assumes L>=2, hence at least one bond; its finite summation is not an empty-chain claim.')
F('def-potts-open-chain-clock-shift-representation','For Q2,3,4 the clock/shift matrices define L one-site generators, L-1 nearest-pair generators and H with2L-1 terms.',N('Only Q2,3,4 are allowed, so sqrt(Q) is nonzero and the normalization never divides by0; Q0 is outside the Definition.'),C('At L1 there is one odd generator and no even bond generator, with H=-e1.'),C('The three q values are distinct normalizations; Q4 has q1 and loop2, without an erroneous root-only restriction.'),C('Even generators stop at j<L; the finite-range formula includes the last odd site but no wrap-around bond.'),N('Clock/shift matrices are explicitly indexed by residues0,...,Q-1; no eigenbasis selection or arbitrary-family choice occurs.'),'The chain input is L>=1, but at L1 the even-generator index range is empty and must contribute no term.')
D['def-potts-open-chain-clock-shift-representation']['cases']['empty']=C('For L1, the even-generator family1<=j<L is empty while the odd family still contains e1; the Hamiltonian has2L-1=1 term.')
F('def-temperley-lieb-diagram-tower-and-right-closure','Appending a strand and exterior right closure define iota_n,c_n for n>=1; E_n=delta inverse times c_n is only defined for nonzero delta.',N('Unnormalized c_n needs no inverse, but normalized E_n explicitly requires delta nonzero; a normalized delta0 closure is excluded.'),C('The first defined map is iota1:TL1 to TL2; its final generator is d1 and closure returns to the scalar rank-one algebra.'),C('A positive conditional expectation is not assumed by this Definition; real-delta reflection and extra faithful C*-trace hypotheses are separate.'),C('The exterior arc joins exactly the rightmost top/bottom pair. The Definition starts n1 and does not create d0.'),N('Each appended or closed diagram is determined by its finite matching; no arbitrary representatives or sections are selected.'),'The tower range here is n>=1, so this Definition does not assert a map indexed by empty rank0.')
F('lem-xxz-open-chain-local-matrix-satisfies-temperley-lieb','Finite mixed-spin matrix multiplication proves square and adjacent triple relations, and disjoint tensor factors commute.',C('The square computation uses no division by delta and explicitly covers q=±i where delta0.'),C('For L2 only one local operator exists and its square relation suffices; adjacent triples have no valid index.'),C('The operators need not be self-adjoint at unit-circle q, but this does not affect the polynomial TL identities proved on spin sectors.'),C('Only valid adjacent indices act on three successive sites; disjoint supports prove commutation for actual pairs in1,...,L-1.'),N('The four invariant spin sectors and their matrices are specified, so all computations are finite without a selection principle.'),'The source Definition assumes L>=2; no zero-site representation is asserted by these embedded local matrices.')
F('thm-xxz-open-chain-hamiltonian-and-boundary-field','Negating the L-1 local Pauli terms telescopes the spin-Z difference to the two open endpoints.',C('When sin(gamma)=0, including q1, the imaginary endpoint coefficient vanishes; the theorem does not retain a parameter-independent boundary field.'),C('At L2 the sum has one bond and no interior Z term to cancel; the endpoint difference is Z1-Z2.'),C('Values cos(gamma)=0 are allowed and remove the ZZ/scalar bulk pieces without invalidating the telescoping identity.'),C('The sum ends at L-1, leaving Z1-ZL and no bond(L,1); interior sites2,...,L-1 cancel pairwise.'),N('This is a specified finite operator sum; no states, roots or infinite spectral choices are made.'),'L>=2 keeps the bond sum nonempty. The regex match to a sum does not extend the claim to L0 or L1.')
F('lem-potts-q2-q3-q4-matrices-satisfy-temperley-lieb','Finite geometric sums identify the generators as sqrt(Q) times one-site or equality projections, whose contractions yield the TL relations.',N('Q is restricted to2,3,4, so Q and sqrt(Q) in projection formulas are nonzero; there is no Q0 branch.'),C('At L1 the sole odd projection gives its square relation; no nonexistent even neighbor or adjacent triple must be checked.'),C('The Q4 parameter q1 is explicitly included; the proof uses finite clock roots rather than a nontrivial-root assumption.'),C('Odd indices run through all L sites, even indices through L-1 pairs, and the support argument uses only actual adjacent operators.'),N('The vectors |+> and the equality projector are given finite sums in a fixed basis; no arbitrary spectral projection is selected.'),'For L1 the even-generator range is empty; relations indexed by a missing neighbor are vacuous, not extra operators.')
D['lem-potts-q2-q3-q4-matrices-satisfy-temperley-lieb']['cases']['empty']=C('The source chain allows L1; its even-generator set and all adjacent-pair relation indices are empty, leaving only the proved odd square relation.')
F('lem-temperley-lieb-diagram-tower-conditional-expectation-identities','Component counts prove algebraic normalized closure identities, with positivity only under the additional faithful finite-dimensional C*-trace regime.',N('The algebraic Statement assumes delta nonzero before E_n divides by it; no delta0 normalized expectation is asserted.'),C('At n1, appending the vertical strand and closing d1 in TL2 give the first unit/tower and Markov formulas.'),C('The positive conclusion requires real nonzero delta, a compatible C*-structure and faithful positive traces; the algebraic formulas alone do not assert positivity.'),C('The last generator is d_n in TL_(n+1) for n>=1, and closing its last strand gives delta inverse times the n-strand identity.'),N('The component comparisons are determined by input matchings; the positivity proof uses the spectral projection of a given finite matrix, not arbitrary-family choice.'),'The stated map indices start n1; no d0 or rank-minus-one target occurs.')
F('lem-temperley-lieb-right-closure-preserves-trace-radical','Cyclicity gives two-sided ideals and trace-preserving bimodularity carries them forward under inclusion and backward under normalized closure.',N('Delta nonzero is a hypothesis of E_n, so its inverse is never formed at0.'),C('At n1 the normalized scalar trace has zero radical; the ideal computations still apply without an elementary generator in TL1.'),C('Trace pairings may be degenerate: the ideal argument is specifically for their radicals and does not assume faithfulness or semisimplicity.'),C('The maps are iota_n:J_n into J_(n+1) and E_n:J_(n+1) into J_n for n>=1; no negative or undefined rank appears.'),N('All test elements are quantified universally, and the proof composes given maps without choosing radical representatives.'),'This Statement starts n1 and does not assert a rank0-indexed normalized closure map.')
A('thm-temperley-lieb-root-quotient-tower-interface','Complete retained path units and trace radical equality produce the actual positive root quotient tower and its faithful positive right expectation.',empty=C('The last proof paragraph explicitly retains the unique scalar path algebras in ranks0 and1 and their unital inclusion.'),zero=C('Root delta=2cos(pi/ell) is positive for ell>=3; absent endpoint path blocks and the omitted [ell] contribution are0, not denominators to invert.'),one=C('At ell3 each retained vertex has one neighbor and the expectation coefficient is1; low levels0,1 are scalar.'),degenerate=C('The boundary ideal is removed before using the positive path trace. The unreduced trace can be degenerate; positivity is proved for the quotient with its reflected matrix adjoint.'),endpoints=C('The current final step5.1 handles a0 via [0]=0 and the top boundary via [ell]=0; only valid retained neighbors contribute.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('Finite positive numbers lambda_a admit their positive square roots, giving specified normalized path isometries; no simultaneous choice of arbitrary intertwiners or unrelated C*-models is made.')})
F('def-extremal-finite-index-ii1-subfactor-data','Ordinary module-trace index is defined before finite frames, and extremality uses the right-action trace on N prime intersect M, not the automatic left M trace.',C('For the general normal N-module H, the zero module has the zero projection model and dimension0; L2(M) of a normalized unital factor is nonzero.'),C('The identity inclusion is allowed and dim_N L2(N)=1 is explicitly normalized; no index-zero subfactor inclusion is admitted.'),C('No separability or irreducibility is assumed. Finite bounded frames and projection models are consequences, not extra hypotheses in ordinary finite index.'),N('Index finiteness and an inclusion are defined, with no ordered interval or spectral endpoint assertion.'),C('AC is explicitly assumed for the standard-form and Hilbert-module amplification suppliers; an arbitrary index set I in the projection model is not a secretly finite basis.'),'Normalized factor trace has tau(1)=1, so the factors are nonzero; a general auxiliary zero Hilbert module uses an empty or zero projection amplification.')
D['def-extremal-finite-index-ii1-subfactor-data']['cases']['empty']=C('The auxiliary amplification notation allows an empty index family for the zero Hilbert module, with dimension0. The subfactor itself has a normalized nonzero unit and is never empty.')
F('cex-xxz-unit-circle-generator-not-self-adjoint','The fixed q=exp(i*pi/4) mixed-spin diagonal entries interchange under conjugation and are unequal, disproving universal self-adjointness.',N('The fixed q and its inverse are nonzero, and the failure comes from nonreal diagonal entries, not a division at0.'),N('This witness is a fixed two-spin operator at q=exp(i*pi/4); no separate q1 self-adjoint example is asserted here.'),C('The non-self-adjoint block is the intended counterexample, despite q being on the unit circle.'),N('The witness fixes a parameter rather than taking a limit or testing the open-chain endpoints.'),N('The displayed standard-basis block and its conjugate transpose require no eigenbasis or arbitrary selection.'),'The non-self-adjointness witness acts on two spins, not an empty tensor product.')
F('rem-temperley-lieb-scaling-limit-and-boundary-status','The remark distinguishes exact finite open chains from source-qualified conjectural/heuristic continuum statements and separate boundary algebras.',N('It supplies no numeric zero-parameter limit theorem or denominator-based operation to check.'),N('It does not formulate a rank-one or one-site spectral claim; it limits the scope of the finite models and cited continuum discussion.'),C('The absence of a universal spectral-gap or Bethe-completeness result is explicit; heuristic evidence is not promoted to a theorem.'),C('Both finite sums omit the last-to-first bond; periodic, blob and two-boundary generators require separate data.'),N('This source-scope comparison selects no arbitrary states or continuum subsequences.'),'No empty-chain or empty-spectrum theorem is asserted in this source-status remark.')
F('ex-matrix-amplification-basic-construction','For P tensor M_k with k>=2 the explicit orthonormal module frame has k squared elements, gives extremality and first basic trace coefficient k inverse squared.',N('K>=2 excludes k0 and makes 1/k and 1/k squared valid; a normalized II1 factor is nonzero.'),N('The selected amplification assumes k>=2, so the identity amplification k1 is not claimed as an input here.'),C('The inclusion is not assumed irreducible: its relative commutant is1 tensor M_k, and the proof computes its right-action trace to establish extremality.'),N('The integer amplification size k>=2 is not an interval with a limiting endpoint or a varying Jones-tower stage claim.'),C('AC is inherited explicitly from the tracial/module/Jones suppliers; the displayed k squared frame itself is a finite specified matrix-unit family.'),'The selected frame has k squared>=4 vectors, and the normalized factor has no empty-module inclusion branch.')
F('ex-potts-q-two-three-four-open-chain','The explicit projection generators give loop/parameter pairs for Q2,3,4 and exactly2L-1 open terms.',N('The three allowed Q values are positive, so sqrt(Q) inverses are well-defined and no Q0 model is asserted.'),C('For L1 there is one odd term and zero even terms, so2L-1=1 is the actual minimum chain count.'),C('Q4 gives q1 rather than a nontrivial root, while the same projection formulas and count remain valid.'),C('No even generator for j=L and no closing boundary term is added; the count is L plus L-1.'),N('The three fixed projection formulas and index counts are finite explicit calculations.'),'At L1 the even-bond sum is empty while the full open Hamiltonian still has its one odd term.')
D['ex-potts-q-two-three-four-open-chain']['cases']['empty']=C('At the allowed L1 boundary, the even-generator index family is empty and contributes0; the odd family contributes1, agreeing with2L-1.')
F('ex-temperley-lieb-rank-three-right-closure','All five rank-three matching basis diagrams are closed explicitly by E2, with the appropriate delta inverse coefficients and normalized trace.',N('Delta nonzero is assumed throughout so the displayed delta inverse and delta inverse squared values do not claim a delta0 normalization.'),C('E2(1_3)=1_2 is the unit case, while closing the sole final cup-cap d2 gives delta inverse times1_2.'),C('The formula makes no positivity claim at arbitrary complex delta; it is algebraic normalized closure on five basis diagrams.'),C('The exterior arc closes the rightmost third strand and sends d2 to the rank-two identity; d1 remains the left generator.'),N('The five matching diagrams and their images are listed, not chosen from an arbitrary basis family.'),'The Example fixes rank3 to rank2 closure and does not add an empty-rank input.')
A('ex-hh-rank-one-hecke-specializations-at-q-i','At q=i the shifted generator is a nonzero square-zero dual-number element, while q=±1 has two complementary idempotents; these are three fixed calculations, not a universal iff specialization criterion.')
A('prop-yangian-gl-n-rtt-hopf-structure','Coefficientwise finite matrix products define the coproduct and recursive formal inverse defines the antipode, proving the Hopf laws.',empty=C('The coefficient of u^0 is the identity matrix and its inverse uses the empty product as1. At every positive degree only finitely many exponent splits and1<=k<=N terms occur; no empty tensor-factor algebra is introduced.'))
A('prop-yangian-rational-r-matrix-yang-baxter-and-invertibility','Flip identities prove Yang-Baxter for all spectral values; the inverse is only away from ±i and their kernels are computed.',endpoints=C('The excluded inverse parameters x=i and x=-i are explicitly covered by the symmetric/antisymmetric projector kernel and rank calculation in step3.1; they are algebraic singular values, not hidden interval endpoints.'))
A('lem-yangian-gl2-one-root-bethe-cancellation','The unwanted term i(a(v)-d(v))/(u-v) cancels when the single Bethe equation holds and the Bethe vector is nonzero.',zero=N('The actual Statement restricts u to D excluding v, and proof1.1 sets x=u-v nonzero before division. The eigenvector also requires B(v)Omega nonzero; no zero-difference or zero-vector conclusion is asserted.'))
A('lem-rational-cherednik-log-connection-meridian-eigenvalues','Flat sector transport makes endpoint-adjusted meridians at different radii conjugate and Gronwall gives the constant-residue characteristic polynomial.',empty=C('The finite vector space E may have dimension0; the unique transport and endpoint automorphism on0 give the degree-zero characteristic polynomial1, so the comparison is vacuous and consistent.'),one=C('For one-dimensional E the limiting operator is the scalar g times exp(i*theta*R), exactly the formula compared by continuity.'),degenerate=C('The residue need not be diagonalizable and eigenvalues may coincide. The Statement asserts equality of characteristic polynomials, not a resonant diagonalizing gauge.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The proof chooses two positive radii and solves specified finite-dimensional ODEs; these are finite selections on the given disk, not an arbitrary-family basis or path choice.')})
A('def-rational-cherednik-reflection-data-and-parameters','Finite complex reflections and the contragredient rank-one correction define the root products and conjugacy-invariant c.',empty=C('W may be the trivial group and then S is empty. In dimension0 the vector space and all root-indexed sums are empty, with the zero-dimensional symplectic form.'),zero=C('Parameters c_s may all be0; nonzero alpha_s is required only for an actual reflection s, so it is never divided out at0.'),one=C('The rank-one image of1-s fixes each alpha_s line and its inverse-scaling partner; a one-dimensional reflection representation is allowed.'),degenerate=C('Rescaling alpha_s by a nonzero scalar inversely rescales alpha_s dual, leaving every product in the presentation unchanged.'),endpoints=N('The data have a finite set of reflections and complex parameters, not an ordered interval or limiting endpoint.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('One nonzero eigenform is chosen for each member of the finite reflection set; its dual is uniquely determined and the products are choice-independent. This is finite selection, not unrestricted choice.')})
A('def-rational-cherednik-algebra','The tensor smash-product quotient imposes commuting x,y blocks and the mixed reflection commutator; associativity is by quotient and PBW is a separate claim.',empty=C('If W is trivial the reflection sum is empty and [y,x]=t*x(y). With dimension0 the tensor algebra is C, consistently yielding the scalar quotient.'),zero=C('All t and c, including t0 and c0, are allowed in the presentation; no inverse parameter appears. Category O is later restricted to t1.'),one=C('In dimension1 with W trivial, the sole mixed relation is [y,x]=t; the order-two reflection gives [y,x]=t-2cs.'),degenerate=C('The Definition intentionally makes no noncollapse or PBW assertion for the quotient; those are not inferred from its formal presentation.'),endpoints=N('The scalars t and c range over C without an ordered endpoint; no limiting specialization is required to define the quotient.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('Once the reflection data are supplied, the ideal is generated by the displayed relations and involves no new choice of representatives or bases.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-commutator-jacobi-compatibility','Equivariance reindexes the finite reflection sum and the alternating trilinear identity on each two-dimensional reflection image kills its Jacobi term.',empty=C('For trivial W the reflection sum is empty, leaving only the central scalar omega term whose cyclic brackets vanish. Dimension0 is also included.'),zero=C('At c0 all reflection terms vanish, while t0 simply removes the central term; the same displayed Jacobi equality remains valid.'),one=C('Each actual reflection has rank1 on h and rank2 on h plus its dual, including a one-dimensional reflection representation.'),degenerate=C('Projection to the reflection-image plane kills the radical contributions; the argument does not require generic c or a nonzero coefficient at that reflection.'),endpoints=N('The lemma quantifies finite group data and vectors, with no interval or spectral endpoint.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The kernel/image decomposition for a supplied finite-order reflection determines the two-dimensional argument; no infinite family of projections is chosen.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-pbw-theorem','A terminating word-reduction measure and joinable group/Jacobi overlaps prove the ordered monomials independent for all t,c.',endpoints=N('All complex t,c are allowed without a limit. The proof is coefficientwise algebraic and no endpoint or nonzero-parameter restriction is introduced.'))
A('def-rational-cherednik-category-o','Category O requires polynomial-finite modules and locally nilpotent commuting y action at t1; it does not assume semisimplicity.',empty=C('The zero module meets finite-generation and local-nilpotence conditions; at dimension0 the category is the corresponding finite-dimensional scalar representation category.'),zero=C('The zero parameter c is allowed at t1. Every vector condition also holds trivially for the zero module; t0 is outside this Definition.'),one=C('For dim(h)=1 the augmentation condition is local nilpotence of the one generator y; the finite-basis equivalence specializes directly.'),degenerate=C('Finite generation and local nilpotence impose no semisimplicity assumption, so exceptional nonsemisimple parameters remain in category O.'),endpoints=N('No ordered parameter interval or limit occurs; t is fixed1 and c is an arbitrary conjugacy-invariant complex function.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('Membership is a pair of conditions on a given module; the definition makes no global selection of generating lists or nilpotence bounds.')})
A('def-rational-cherednik-standard-module','Induction from a finite W-module with y action0 defines Delta(E), and PBW identifies its polynomial tensor space for reducible as well as irreducible E.',empty=C('E=0 is allowed and induction gives Delta(0)=0. For h0 the polynomial tensor factor is C.'),zero=C('The inducing y action is0 by definition, and c0 remains an allowed algebra parameter without changing the induction construction.'),one=C('A one-dimensional W-label is permitted; the trivial one gives the later polynomial representation.'),degenerate=C('E need not be irreducible. Direct sums induce to direct sums, and a unique simple head is not stipulated for a reducible label.'),endpoints=N('This finite-dimensional induction has no ordered label endpoint or analytic limiting parameter.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The tensor induction is specified for the supplied W-module, and the PBW identification does not require selecting a basis to define the module.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-standard-module-lies-in-category-o','Finite polynomial generators come from E and each y lowers polynomial degree, so sufficiently long y monomials kill each vector.',empty=C('For E0 the induced module is0 and both category-O conditions are vacuous; degree0 polynomials are killed by every y.'),one=C('A one-dimensional label supplies one polynomial generator; the degree-lowering proof works unchanged.'),degenerate=C('No irreducibility of E is used: only its finite dimension and the fact that all y kill the inducing space are needed.'),endpoints=C('At polynomial degree0 the y action is0; a degree-d vector is killed by any product of more than d y generators, giving the actual low-degree boundary.'))
A('def-rational-cherednik-polynomial-representation','The inducing label is the one-dimensional trivial W-module and its standard induction is the polynomial representation, with Dunkl formula proved separately.',empty=C('For h0 and trivial W the polynomial ring is C and the representation is its scalar module, not an undefined empty induction.'),zero=C('At c0 the later reflection correction vanishes; the induced Definition itself is valid for every c and always has y kill the inducing constant.'),one=C('The label has dimension1 and the constant polynomial is the generating vector; this does not restrict the dimension of h to1.'),degenerate=C('No simplicity is asserted by this Definition; the polynomial standard may have proper submodules at special c.'),endpoints=N('The construction fixes t1 and arbitrary c, without an interval or a limiting parameter claim.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The trivial label and tensor induction are specified canonical objects; no arbitrary irreducible representative is chosen here.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-category-o-has-finite-length','Euler weights and polynomial Noetherianity give finite composition series, while unique simple heads are proved only for irreducible labels.',endpoints=N('The finite-length Statement has no ordered interval or limit. The lower bounds of Euler progressions are internal to the proof, not extra parameter endpoints.'))
A('lem-rational-cherednik-polynomial-representation-dunkl-formula','Divisibility of f-sf by its hyperplane form makes Dunkl operators polynomial; Jacobi and the differential centralizer prove commutation.',endpoints=N('Dunkl action is a polynomial identity in arbitrary c and acts on every polynomial degree, with no ordered or asymptotic endpoint.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The proof uses the given finite reflection data and the polynomial generator1; the displayed operator is independent of eigenform rescaling and makes no new arbitrary choice.')})
A('lem-finite-modules-with-connection-over-a-polynomial-open-are-locally-free','Nakayama lifts a finite fiber basis and a least-order nonzero relation contradicts repeated Leibniz differentiation, giving local freeness without integrability.',empty=C('For N0 the fiber rank r0 branch in step1.1 gives the zero free module. Dimension n0 reduces to finite-dimensional vector spaces over C.'),zero=C('The defining f must be a nonzero polynomial and the point satisfies f(p) nonzero, so coefficients of regular germs have no vanishing local denominator. Zero module and rank0 are separately allowed.'),one=C('The rank-one fiber case lifts one generator; the same least-order relation argument proves that its local module is free of rank1.'),degenerate=C('Integrability is explicitly unnecessary for local freeness. Stable submodules and quotients inherit Leibniz operators even beyond category O.'),endpoints=N('The input is a point in a principal open, not a boundary value where f vanishes; no extension across that excluded divisor is asserted.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('At one fixed point the proof lifts a finite fiber basis and clears finitely many local denominators; it does not choose local bases simultaneously at every point.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-dunkl-localization-and-flat-connection','Principal symbols give the injection, inverted reflection forms recover all derivations and finite coherent connections yield exact flat-bundle fibers.',one=C('A one-dimensional localized fiber is covered by the same coherent-connection proof; exactness is a fiber statement after the quotient bundle becomes locally free.'),degenerate=C('Localized rank can be0 for torsion modules; they yield the zero flat bundle. There is no generic-c assumption in the algebra localization or local-freeness argument.'),endpoints=N('The hyperplane divisor is excluded by inverting delta, so the flat bundle is asserted only on h_reg and not across its boundary.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The proof fixes finitely many coordinate generators to compare symbols and apply local freeness. It chooses no simultaneous family of parallel frames or hyperplane-avoiding paths.')})
A('def-rational-cherednik-kz-functor-and-finite-hecke-algebra','The braid functor and meridian quotient use the positive fractional lift, conormal generator endpoint and internal residue roots; no Hecke dimension theorem is built in.',empty=C('With no reflecting hyperplanes the meridian relation family is empty and its product imposes no relation; for trivial W the same local-system construction remains meaningful.'),zero=C('At c0 each r_(H,j)=0 and mu_(H,j)=zeta_H^j. The j0 root is always1; no division by a vanishing parameter occurs.'),one=C('The fixed character j0 has r0=0 and mu0=1. Reflection orders are at least2; an order-one stabilizer is not included as a reflecting hyperplane.'),degenerate=C('Coincident mu roots are allowed in the defining polynomial; the Definition assumes neither semisimplicity nor diagonalizable resonant monodromy.'),endpoints=C('Characters run through j0,...,e_H-1; a positive normal lift ends at s_H inverse and is identified by s_H, with GGOR inverse normal generator explicitly separated.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('A basepoint in the nonempty regular open and finitely many hyperplane generators are fixed. KZ is formed with those choices, rather than asserting a choice-free canonical braid basepoint.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-standard-module-local-connection-residue','The explicit commutator cancellation computes connection d+sum c*dalpha/alpha*(1-s_E) and its cyclic-character residue r_(H,j).',one=C('For the trivial one-dimensional label s_E=1, all reflection correction terms vanish; for a single cyclic character the residue is the displayed scalar r_(H,j).'),degenerate=C('E may be reducible; its finite cyclic restriction decomposes into eigenspaces and the residue formula applies on each without a simple-head premise.'))
A('thm-rational-cherednik-category-o-projective-generator-from-generalized-standards','Uniform Euler bounds make exact weight functors represented by generalized-standard summands, yielding a finite algebra and standard-filtered projective covers.',empty=C('The zero object is detected by the same Hom functors and has an empty presentation; polynomial degree0 and the finite truncated Y unit remain present even when n0.'),zero=C('Every c, including c0, is allowed; exact Euler projections use polynomial primary decomposition, not division by parameter differences assumed nonzero globally.'),one=C('Each irreducible simple label has its projective cover with one-dimensional Hom to that simple; one-dimensional W-labels are included in the same construction.'),degenerate=C('Equal Euler scalars are collected in one generalized eigensummand, so resonance and nonsemisimple category O are not excluded.'),endpoints=C('The truncation uses degrees0 through n, including the unit degree0; the uniform bound kills all higher Y degrees before the representing functor is used.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The proof chooses a sufficiently large finite n and finite generating/basis data; its indecomposable and standard-flag constructions use finite-dimensional modules, not a basis choice for an arbitrary family.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-kz-relation-specializes-in-flat-family','A fixed polynomial-free generalized-standard family admits nonresonant gauges; holomorphic monodromy extends its exact meridian relation to all parameters.',empty=C('The n0 inducing space still contains C[W] and its degree0 unit. If the reflection set is empty, there are no meridian polynomials to test.'),zero=C('C0 lies in the family and all residue differences there are0; excluded gauge resonances are nonzero integers, and the final holomorphic identity holds at c0 regardless.'),one=C('The normalized gauge has constant coefficient I; the first meridian root mu_(H,0) is1, with every character included in its full polynomial.'),degenerate=C('At resonant parameters the polynomial relation is obtained by holomorphic continuation, not by claiming monodromy is diagonalizable or relations pass from composition factors.'),endpoints=C('The finite Y truncation includes degree0 and every meridian includes all j0,...,e_H-1; endpoint s_H is built into the equivariant normalized gauge.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('A finite inducing space, finitely many meridian paths and one nonexceptional line direction are fixed; the dense-set argument removes countably many points from that complex line and does not select witnesses for an arbitrary family.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-kz-standard-modules-factor-through-hecke','Standards are summands of finite sums of the degree0 generalized regular standard, so the exact all-parameter meridian polynomial passes to them.',empty=C('E0 gives the zero standard and zero KZ fiber, on which each polynomial relation is vacuous. With no hyperplanes there are no relations to impose.'),zero=C('The exact polynomial identity holds for all c, including0, and for a zero KZ fiber; no root or parameter is inverted.'),one=C('A one-dimensional W-label is a finite regular-label summand and has its same-dimensional localized fiber; the argument includes the j0 root1.'),degenerate=C('E need not be irreducible and resonant monodromy need not be diagonalizable. The proof supplies an annihilating polynomial, stronger than only listing eigenvalues.'),endpoints=C('The degree0 generalized standard and the full finite character range0<=j<e_H are retained; no exceptional endpoint label is omitted.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('Finite-group averaging splits the given finite E from a finite free regular representation; there is no need to select splittings for all W-modules simultaneously.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-kz-factors-through-hecke-algebra','A finite sum of sufficiently large generalized standards surjects onto an arbitrary object, and exact KZ carries their meridian relation to its quotient.',empty=C('For M0, its KZ fiber is0 and factorization is tautological; an empty hyperplane set imposes no meridian relations.'),zero=C('All c, including0, are retained. A torsion module may have zero KZ fiber, and the relation still holds without a faithfulness assumption.'),one=C('For a one-dimensional nonzero KZ fiber, each defining meridian polynomial still annihilates the scalar operator by the same quotient argument.'),degenerate=C('The proof handles nonsemisimple objects and resonant parameters using a generating surjection, not the false inference from polynomials on simple subquotients.'),endpoints=N('There is no ordered parameter interval; the target is the internally defined meridian quotient at the fixed arbitrary c.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('For one M and c a sufficiently large finite generalized standard and finite surjection are chosen; no uniform choice over all parameters is asserted.')})
A('lem-cherednik-nilpotent-induced-connections-have-moderate-growth','A finite nilpotent inducing filtration gives triangular line ODEs with uniformly bounded residue orders, and rational quotient frames transfer power growth.',empty=C('For M0 choose the zero inducing module; its localized sections are0. A reflection-free arrangement has no finite reflecting punctures, while infinity is still treated.'),zero=C('The zero augmentation action is the standard-module case. At c0 the residue blocks vanish, and the finite nilpotent off-diagonal terms still give only power growth.'),one=C('A one-dimensional quotient or determinant line satisfies the same scalar power bound; there is no separate rank-at-least2 assumption.'),degenerate=C('Quotients and duals are controlled using rational generating minors and the finite filtration, without requiring a logarithmic trivialization at infinity.'),endpoints=C('Every finite puncture and infinity on bounded-angle sectors are included; line-dependent radii and constants are allowed while exponents are uniform.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The proof chooses one finite generating list, its finite nilpotent orbit span and a nonzero finite minor for a rational frame; it does not make choices for an arbitrary family of lines.')})
A('lem-bounded-separate-rationality-is-rational','Degree-bounded line restrictions are interpolated at2D+1 nodes, then a local holomorphic identity theorem propagates the resulting meromorphic equality.',empty=N('U is explicitly a nonempty Zariski open. The n0 case is one point and a constant, not an empty domain; the function-family hypothesis does not permit U empty.'),zero=C('D0 uses one interpolation node; the proof shows Q nonzero by the same root-count argument. The zero function may be represented with numerator0 and denominator1.'),one=C('For n1 the line-rationality hypothesis is the conclusion, and for D0 there is exactly one node as stated in the proof.'),degenerate=C('The interpolating Q may vanish at some nodes; cross multiplication P*Qstar-Pstar*Q has all2D+1 zeros and handles those vanishing coefficients without illegal division.'),endpoints=C('Poles are permitted meromorphically on U; the conclusion is equality where the rational function is defined, not a claim of extending holomorphically across every pole.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The proof chooses finitely many distinct nodes inside a holomorphic neighborhood and solves one finite homogeneous linear system; it does not choose linewise rational expressions for an arbitrary family.')})
A('thm-cherednik-localized-monodromy-is-fully-faithful-with-subquotient-closed-image','Moderate-growth algebraization of horizontal maps and determinant ratios gives full faithfulness and algebraic invariant subbundles, with graph roofs realizing the torsion quotient.',empty=C('The zero localized bundle and zero invariant plane are included; rank0 or full-rank invariant subspaces are separated before the determinant-line argument.'),zero=C('A map with zero localization has torsion image and is0 in the quotient. At arbitrary c, zero localized ranks require no denominator or nonzero-fiber inference.'),one=C('An invariant line is the r1 determinant case; its scalar monodromy is removed by a local power twist before rational coordinate ratios are taken.'),degenerate=C('Resonant parameters and nonsemisimple monodromy remain allowed; power growth plus scalar twisting replaces an unwarranted global diagonalization assumption.'),endpoints=C('The regular open excludes the reflection divisor; finite punctures and infinity are controlled on generic lines before proving rational regularity on all of that open.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('A fixed intertwiner or invariant finite plane is transported; finitely many rational frames and one complex logarithm for a scalar monodromy are chosen, not a simultaneous arbitrary-family Riemann-Hilbert construction.')})
A('lem-rational-cherednik-costandard-orthogonality-and-character','Euler-finite coinduction, finite Koszul orthogonality and triangular characters give the simple socle and standard composition character for irreducible E.',empty=C('When h0 the Koszul complex has only its degree0 term and the character is the inducing label. No nonzero higher-degree term is manufactured.'),zero=C('At polynomial/exterior total degree0 the augmentation is used instead of dividing by p+i in the contracting homotopy; c0 is permitted throughout.'),one=C('For a one-dimensional irreducible E, the lowest Euler space is that label and the same Hom dimension1 proves the simple socle occurs once.'),degenerate=C('E is irreducible, but category O need not be semisimple. Equal standard/costandard K0 classes assert multiplicities, not module isomorphism or a real-reflection duality.'),endpoints=C('The Euler-locally-finite part uses the direct sum of degrees d>=0, not the full coinduced product, and the separate total-degree0 Koszul term handles its lower endpoint.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The coinduction and Koszul maps are specified algebraic formulas; the proof explicitly uses no infinite free-module basis or arbitrary choice principle.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-category-o-bgg-reciprocity-and-projective-generator-rank','Standard/costandard Hom-Ext orthogonality proves BGG multiplicities, and exact KZ is represented by finite projectives with endomorphism rank sum(dimE)^2=|W|.',empty=C('Simple labels with r_E=dim KZ(L(E))=0 contribute no projective summand; the functor still represents zero values on torsion objects.'),zero=C('Some r_E may vanish and are explicitly allowed. No dimension or freeness statement for the Hecke algebra is inferred from this calculation.'),one=C('Each simple has scalar endomorphisms; lifting a basis of its r_E-dimensional KZ value gives the natural representing map, including r_E=1.'),degenerate=C('The reciprocity and rank argument works at every c using finite length, projective flags and exact KZ, not generic semisimplicity.'),endpoints=N('The theorem is a finite sum over Irr(W), not an ordered or asymptotic parameter limit.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('For the finite irreducible-label set, finitely many basis vectors of KZ(L(E)) are chosen and lifted to KZ(P(E)); these are finite-dimensional choices only.')})
A('thm-rational-cherednik-kz-quotient-equivalence-under-bmr-rank','The subobject-closed KZ image forces a surjection from the internal Hecke algebra to the projective endomorphism algebra; the explicit equal-dimension hypothesis makes it an isomorphism.',empty=C('Torsion objects become zero in the Serre quotient and zero KZ modules remain included; the functor is not claimed faithful on all objects of O before quotienting.'),zero=C('The proof identifies the kernel exactly as torsion, including zero fibers, and imposes the Hecke rank equality separately for the given arbitrary parameter.'),one=C('The image subalgebra in the regular endomorphism algebra contains its unit1; subobject closure then forces that image to be the whole algebra.'),degenerate=C('Neither Hecke nor category O semisimplicity is assumed. The numerical rank hypothesis cannot replace the separate surjectivity proof.'),endpoints=N('This is a conditional algebraic equivalence at fixed c, not a limiting statement at a root or interval endpoint.'),**{'nonempty-choice':C('The quotient generator has finite presentations and finite Hom spaces; lifting maps uses projectivity and does not choose representatives for all Serre-quotient roofs at once.')})
A('ex-rational-cherednik-trivial-group-is-the-weyl-algebra','For the trivial group and dimh1 the empty reflection sum gives [y,x]=1 and the polynomial action is multiplication and differentiation.',zero=C('There is no reflection parameter when W is trivial, so c0 is the empty parameter function; the commutator remains1 at t1, not0.'),endpoints=N('This example fixes dimh1 and t1, and makes no limiting-parameter or polynomial-degree cutoff claim.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('A single dual coordinate pair is fixed and the reflection sum is empty; all operator computations are explicit on polynomials.')})
A('cex-rational-cherednik-category-o-not-semisimple-at-half','At rank1,c1/2, xC[x] is a proper invariant submodule and any one-dimensional complement would give a nonzero polynomial eigenvector of multiplication by x.',empty=N('The witness W=Z/2 and polynomial module C[x] are nonzero; an empty module or reflection set cannot witness this nonsplit sequence.'),zero=C('The quotient has x=y0 and s1, while y*x=0 makes the submodule stable. Nonzero c1/2 is fixed, so a c0 semisimplicity assertion is not part of this example.'),**{'nonempty-choice':N('The witness submodule and quotient are explicit. A hypothetical complement is refuted by the integral-domain equation(x-lambda)f=0, without selecting a family of complements.')})
